## What this repository is

The operational substrate of {{ ship.title or polity.title or "this polity" }}. The repository carries:

- A novel mathematical theory of *predication categories* (bilattice values, predication fibrations, closure 2-monad, four-regime classification).
- A novel engineering theory of *predicated computing* built on top of it ({% for st in predicated_computing_subtheories %}{{ st.slug }}{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %}).
- An operational layer (command, engineering, science) that runs the ship.

The theories are **novel** — they don't exist in published literature in this form. Treat the corpus as research-active, not as an established textbook.

## How to work here

### Reading

- For math questions, start at [`predication-categories/_index.md`](library/theories/humanities/theories/math/theories/categories/theories/predication-categories/_index.md).
- For engineering questions, start at [`predicated-computing/_index.md`](library/theories/engineering/theories/tech/theories/computing/theories/predicated-computing/_index.md). Engineering theorems cite math objects directly — when an engineering claim names a math object, that's the load-bearing reference, follow it.
- Use `pyfars show <slug>` and `pyfars context <slug>` to navigate the predication graph rather than `grep`.

### Writing

- Every note is a Markdown file with YAML frontmatter; one entity per file; lower-kebab-case slugs.
- Frontmatter uses `type:` (kind of note: concept/object/morphism/theorem/etc.) and `class:` (sub-classification).
- New notes go in the canonical directory per `pyfars where <kind> <slug>`.
- For engineering theorems and objects, math is **first-class in `depends-on:`**. Cite math objects directly; don't add a "Connection to math reformulation" sidebar — build the math into the body.
- Don't write notes you don't understand. Lifting wisdom across layers is a research claim, not a mechanical cross-reference.
- Read each file before moving it out of `triage/`. Bulk-lifts have repeatedly produced disasters.

### Editing

- The substrate's `triage/` directories (e.g. `operations/engineering/triage/`) are workspaces; every file in them must keep a `triage/` segment in its path. Enrich content rather than restructuring to remove the triage marker.
- Don't add `locates:` or `department:` frontmatter when the path already conveys it.

### Committing

- Don't commit unless the user asks you to.
- When you do commit, use `git add` with specific paths — never `git add -A` unless the user explicitly says to commit everything in flight.
- Commit messages should describe **what changed and why**. Reference task records by their slug (e.g. `task-15`, not `Task #15`) so `pyfars audit-commits` can bind the commit to a witness.

### Validation

- Run `pyfars validate` periodically on touched files. Many existing diagnostics are pre-existing and not from your work; filter to the files you've actually edited.

### What not to do

- Don't write narratives unless asked; the sprint will produce its own narratives organically.
- Don't add comments to code beyond what's required to convey non-obvious WHY. Don't write multi-line docstrings for code whose purpose is already clear.
- Don't introduce backwards-compatibility shims, feature flags, or "removed once X" TODOs without an explicit reason.
- Don't destroy uncommitted work to avoid pre-commit hook failures or to "clean up." Fix the underlying issue.
- Don't hand-edit harness-generated files (this file, README.md, replit.md). Edit the templates that ship with PyFARS at `src/pyfars/harnesses/repo-instructions/templates/` (in `github.com/emsenn/pyfars`) and re-run `{{ generation_command }}`.

### Audit-output discipline

Audit *outputs* (reports, drift snapshots, coverage rolls, dated audit findings) MUST go in one of these places, and **nowhere else**:

1. The audited theory's own `audits/` subdirectory (e.g. `library/theories/.../<theory>/audits/<class>-<date>.md` with `type: audit, class: <kind>`). Check for a sibling `audits/_index.md` and follow its conventions; add yourself to its index.
2. `operations/engineering/triage/...` somewhere matching the audit's subject (for one-off audits that don't yet have a home theory).

Additional rules:

- Don't scatter `audit-*.md` or `*-audit.md` files into random directories.
- Don't start a dated daily-roll audit series (`audit-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, `coverage-YYYY-MM-DD.md`, etc.) unless the captain explicitly asked for the series.
- An "audit" that's really an FYI or a brain-dump is not an audit; file it as a session-note or a triage idea.
- The substrate already carries audit *skills* under `operations/engineering/skills/<pack>/audit-*/` and audit *agents* (`theory-auditor`, `predication-category-theory-auditor`, etc.) — invoke those rather than rolling your own.

